id: magic-trick
name: Magic Trick
type: standard
actionCost: 1
kiCost: 0
attacking: false
requiresTarget: false
special: true
usageLimit: 1/round
clashSkill: useMagic
clashDefenderSkills: intuition, useMagic
moveSkill: useMagic
movePerRank: 1
magicTrick: true
# Folded, because `coerce` splits a header value on commas and this line has one in it.
exploitable: >
  If you fail the initial Clash, this triggers the Exploit Maneuver from the target.
exploitOnLoss: true
says: >
  This movement does not trigger the Exploit Maneuver.
source: Core Rule
description: >
  Three tricks and you pick one first: Impaired on a won Clash, shoving somebody your
  Use Magic Ranks in Squares, or moving yourself that far with nothing to answer it.
text: >
  Magic Trick Maneuver [1/Round]: You use your mystical prowess to manipulate the
  battlefield, moving yourself and your enemies around at-will.
  –Maneuver Type: Standard Maneuver
  –Action Cost: 1 Action
  –Minions: N/A
  –KP Cost: N/A
  –Exploitable: If you fail the initial Clash, this triggers the Exploit Maneuver from
  the target.
  –Effect: Apply one of the following effects:

  * Target an Opponent within your Melee Range. Make a Clash (Use Magic vs Intuition/Use
  Magic) against them. If you win, the target gains the Impaired Combat Condition until
  the start of your next turn.

  * Target a Character within your Melee Range. Make a Clash (Use Magic vs Intuition/Use
  Magic) against them. If you win, move that target by a number of Squares equal to your
  number of Skill Ranks in the Use Magic Skill.

  * Move a number of Squares equal to your number of Skill Ranks in the Use Magic Skill.
  This movement does not trigger the Exploit Maneuver.

---

# --- The choice comes first ------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Apply one of the following effects", and the three are not three outcomes of one thing:
# two open a Clash and the third does not, and one needs an Opponent where another will
# take any Character. So the question is asked before anything is paid for, which is the
# first Maneuver here to work that way - the Thrust and the Dirty Trick both choose after
# the dice, because what they are choosing between is what winning buys.
#
# --- The Clash ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "(Use Magic vs Intuition/Use Magic)" - a different Skill on each side, and a slash on the
# defender's, which is a choice they make. The same shape the Feint has.
#
# The first two effects make the same Clash and differ only in what winning buys and who
# may be aimed at: an Opponent for the first, any Character for the second. That second one
# is why the Maneuver takes a target rather than an Opponent, and the difference is said on
# the card rather than policed - who counts as an Opponent is the table's.
#
# --- What winning buys ---------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# The first: Impaired "until the start of your next turn", which is your turn - so the clock
# is kept by you and the Condition sits on them, the split Analysis and Dirty Trick use. A
# start edge, which this system counts as always the next one whichever turn you are
# standing in.
#
# The second: a number of Squares off your Use Magic Ranks. Said and not done, like every
# Square here - the number is what the card can give, and the moving is the player's.
#
# --- The third, which is not a Clash -----------------------------------------------------------
#
# Move that far yourself. No Clash, so nothing to fail, so nothing to Exploit - which the
# entry says again on its own line: "this movement does not trigger the Exploit Maneuver".
# Both halves come to the same place here, because the only thing that would have offered
# one is the Exploitable line, and that line is about failing a Clash this effect never
# makes.
#
# --- The Exploitable line -----------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "If you fail the initial Clash, this triggers the Exploit Maneuver from the target." Not
# when the Maneuver is used, which is when every other Exploitable line fires, and not to
# everybody in reach - to the one person it was aimed at, and only on a loss. So the line is
# carried as printed for a reader and `exploitOnLoss` keeps it out of the door that offers
# the rest, which hands it out from the Clash instead.
#
# --- How it is gained --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Use Magic, at the 2+ Ranks every Skill grants a Special Maneuver at - which is also the
# Skill this Maneuver rolls and counts. A coincidence of this entry rather than a rule: the
# Dirty Trick rolls Bluff and Bluff opens the Feint instead.
#
# Use Magic is Required, so no Rank is no roll at all - and this Maneuver counts its Ranks
# for distance, so a character who has just opened it at two Ranks moves two Squares.
#
# Worth noting from that entry: it is the first Skill whose Adventuring Maneuver line reads
# N/A, and its prose says "it lacks a Combat Maneuver due to the skill being used for a
# large amount of effects" - which sits oddly beside the line granting this one, since this
# is a Standard Maneuver used in combat. Built from the stat lines, which are the ones that
# say what a character gets.
